What is a Data Type?
A data type defines the type of value a variable can store in a programming language. It determines:
- What kind of data can be stored (e.g., numbers, text, boolean values).
- The operations that can be performed on that data.
- How much memory is allocated for storage.
Types of Data Types
Data types are broadly categorized into Primitive Data Types and Non-Primitive Data Types.
1. Primitive Data Types
Primitive data types are the most basic types built into a programming language. They hold simple values and have no additional functionalities.
Data Type	Description	Example
Integer (int)	Stores whole numbers	int age = 21;
Floating Point (float, double)	Stores decimal numbers	float price = 99.99;
Character (char)	Stores a single character	char grade = 'A';
Boolean (bool)	Stores true or false values	bool isPassed = true;
String (in some languages)	Stores a sequence of characters	String name = "John"; (in Java)
Example in Python

age = 25  # Integer
pi = 3.14  # Float
grade = 'A'  # Character
is_student = True  # Boolean
name = "Alice"  # String

2. Non-Primitive Data Types
Non-primitive data types (also called reference types) are more complex and can store multiple values or objects.
Data Type	Description	Example
Array	Collection of similar data types	int numbers[] = {1, 2, 3, 4};
List (Dynamic Array in Python)	Resizable collection of elements	list = [1, 2, 3, 4]
Tuple	Immutable ordered collection	tuple_data = (1, 2, 3)
Dictionary (Map/HashMap)	Stores key-value pairs	dict_data = {"name": "John", "age": 25}
Set	Unordered collection of unique elements	set_data = {1, 2, 3, 4}
Class/Object	User-defined data structure	class Person {} (in Java)
Example in Python

# List (Similar to Array)
numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

# Tuple (Immutable list)
coordinates = (10, 20)

# Dictionary (Key-Value pairs)
person = {"name": "Alice", "age": 25}

# Set (Unique elements)
unique_numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}

Key Differences: Primitive vs. Non-Primitive Data Types
Feature	Primitive Data Types	Non-Primitive Data Types
Complexity	Simple	Complex
Storage	Stores a single value	Stores multiple values
Operations	Direct operations (addition, comparison, etc.)	Require methods and functions for manipulation
Example	int a = 10;	int arr[] = {1,2,3};
Conclusion
- Primitive Data Types are the building blocks of data manipulation (e.g., int, float, char).
- Non-Primitive Data Types are more advanced and store multiple values or complex structures (e.g., list, dictionary, class).
